✅ What is JRE (Java Runtime Environment)?
JRE = everything needed to RUN Java programs — but NOT to develop/compile them.

Interview formula:
  JRE = JVM + Core Libraries (+ config / supporting runtime files)

Nested picture:
  JDK
   └── JRE
        └── JVM


🔹 Key Points
- Runs .class / JAR files
- Does NOT include javac, javadoc, jdb, etc.
- Used by end-users / servers where you only need to execute apps
- JDK already contains a JRE (install JDK → you can also run)


📦 JRE Contains

| Component                         | Purpose                                              |
| --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| **JVM**                           | Executes bytecode (.class)                           |
| **Core Libraries**                | APIs: java.lang, java.util, java.io, …               |
| **Configs / policies**            | Security, timezones, logging, etc.                   |
| **Deployment bits (legacy)**      | Applets / Java Web Start — obsolete, ignore in 2020s |

Libraries note:
  Pre–Java 9 → rt.jar
  Java 9+    → modules (no rt.jar)


🧠 How JRE Works

  Java Bytecode (.class / JAR)
              ↓
             JRE
        ↙           ↘
      JVM      Core Libraries
              ↓
         Program Runs

Flow in words:
1) `java MyApp` starts
2) JRE launches JVM
3) ClassLoader loads classes from libraries + your app
4) JVM executes bytecode (interpret + JIT)


⚠️ JDK vs JRE

| Feature            | JDK                      | JRE                |
| ------------------ | ------------------------ | ------------------ |
| Purpose            | Develop + Run            | Only Run           |
| Includes           | JRE + Compiler + Tools   | JVM + Libraries    |
| `javac`            | ✅ Yes                    | ❌ No               |
| Used by            | Developers               | End-users, servers |


✅ Real-World Use
- Developer machine → install JDK (compile + run)
- Production / user machine → need a runtime only
  - historically: separate JRE install
  - today: often JDK, or a custom runtime via `jlink`, or runtime bundled with the app


☑️ Modern note (important in interviews)
Since Java 11, Oracle stopped shipping a separate JRE download —
runtime comes with the JDK (or you build a minimal image with jlink).
Concept of JRE still exists and is still asked: “run-only environment.”


💬 Quick interview Qs

Q: Can JRE compile code?
A: No — no javac.

Q: Can we run without JDK?
A: Yes — if .class/JAR already exists and a JRE/runtime is present.

Q: Relation?
A: JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM


🧠 Interview one-liner
“JRE is the run-only layer: JVM + class libraries. It runs bytecode but cannot compile; the JDK adds development tools on top of the JRE.”
